The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (!pfn_valid(pfn))
    return;

pfn = pte_pfn(pteval);
```
Reason:
The `pfn_valid(pfn)` function checks if the Page Frame Number (PFN) corresponds to a valid page. After the change in semantics, it can return true for pages that are reserved but not mapped by the kernel. This leads to accessing these reserved pages, causing kernel crashes during user-space I/O tests.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (folio_test_reserved(folio))
    return;
```
Reason:
This added check ensures that if the folio (the structure representing a group of pages) is reserved, the function will return early, preventing any further operations on that folio. This effectively avoids accessing reserved pages that could lead to kernel crashes.

